\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {XII}{\ignorespaces Conditioning-mechanism ablation. Both denoisers consume the full 196-token ego sequence and differ only in how they attend to it. Evaluated on the held-out test split (a scene-disjoint half of the validation set), CFG\nobreakspace  {}=\nobreakspace  {}10, mean\nobreakspace  {}$\pm $\nobreakspace  {}95\% CI over 3 replications, best checkpoint each. The two mechanisms are statistically comparable (overlapping FID intervals); cross-attention provides no advantage. The intervals here are over sampling replications at one training seed per arm, and EgoPed\xspace  's seed spread ($\pm 0.79$, Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref {sec:seeds}) is far wider, so this comparison can exclude a large effect but cannot resolve a small one.\relax }}{15}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:xattn}{{XII}{15}{Conditioning-mechanism ablation. Both denoisers consume the full 196-token ego sequence and differ only in how they attend to it. Evaluated on the held-out test split (a scene-disjoint half of the validation set), CFG~=~10, mean~$\pm $~95\% CI over 3 replications, best checkpoint each. The two mechanisms are statistically comparable (overlapping FID intervals); cross-attention provides no advantage. The intervals here are over sampling replications at one training seed per arm, and \method 's seed spread ($\pm 0.79$, Sec.~\ref {sec:seeds}) is far wider, so this comparison can exclude a large effect but cannot resolve a small one.\relax }{table.caption.15}{}}
